'Find orders by optional status, city, and date range' — four implementations.

**1. JPQL (static, all filters required):**

```java
@Query("""
    SELECT o FROM Order o
    WHERE o.status = :status AND o.shippingCity = :city
      AND o.createdAt BETWEEN :from AND :to
    ORDER BY o.createdAt DESC
""")
List<Order> findAll(@Param("status") OrderStatus status,
                    @Param("city") String city,
                    @Param("from") Instant from,
                    @Param("to") Instant to);
```

**2. JPA Criteria API (dynamic, type-safe):**

```java
CriteriaBuilder cb = em.getCriteriaBuilder();
CriteriaQuery<Order> cq = cb.createQuery(Order.class);
Root<Order> o = cq.from(Order.class);

List<Predicate> predicates = new ArrayList<>();
if (status != null) predicates.add(cb.equal(o.get("status"), status));
if (city != null)   predicates.add(cb.equal(o.get("shippingCity"), city));
if (from != null)   predicates.add(cb.greaterThanOrEqualTo(o.get("createdAt"), from));
if (to != null)     predicates.add(cb.lessThanOrEqualTo(o.get("createdAt"), to));

cq.where(predicates.toArray(new Predicate[0]))
  .orderBy(cb.desc(o.get("createdAt")));

List<Order> results = em.createQuery(cq).getResultList();
```

**3. Spring Data Specification (Criteria wrapped):**

```java
Specification<Order> spec = Specification
    .where(status != null ? (r, q, cb) -> cb.equal(r.get("status"), status) : null)
    .and(city != null ? (r, q, cb) -> cb.equal(r.get("shippingCity"), city) : null)
    .and(from != null ? (r, q, cb) -> cb.greaterThanOrEqualTo(r.get("createdAt"), from) : null)
    .and(to != null ? (r, q, cb) -> cb.lessThanOrEqualTo(r.get("createdAt"), to) : null);

List<Order> results = orderRepo.findAll(spec, Sort.by("createdAt").descending());
```

**4. Native SQL (for features JPQL cannot express):**

```java
@Query(value = """
    WITH ranked AS (
      SELECT o.*, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY o.customer_id ORDER BY o.created_at DESC) rn
      FROM orders o WHERE o.created_at >= :cutoff
    )
    SELECT * FROM ranked WHERE rn = 1
""", nativeQuery = true)
List<Order> findLatestOrderPerCustomer(@Param("cutoff") Instant cutoff);
```

The window function (`ROW_NUMBER() OVER PARTITION BY`) cannot be expressed in JPQL — native is the only option.

**Pattern 1 — closed interface projection for list view:**

```java
public interface OrderListItem {
    Long getId();
    OrderStatus getStatus();
    Instant getCreatedAt();
    BigDecimal getTotalAmount();
}

public interface OrderRepository extends JpaRepository<Order, Long> {
    Page<OrderListItem> findByCustomerId(Long customerId, Pageable page);
    // SELECT id, status, created_at, total_amount FROM orders WHERE customer_id = ?
}
```

No entity, no lazy proxies, Jackson serializes the interface proxy directly.

**Pattern 2 — class-based record with explicit join:**

```java
public record OrderSummary(
    Long id,
    OrderStatus status,
    Instant createdAt,
    String customerName,
    BigDecimal total
) {}

public interface OrderRepository extends JpaRepository<Order, Long> {
    @Query("""
        SELECT new com.acme.OrderSummary(o.id, o.status, o.createdAt, c.name, o.totalAmount)
        FROM Order o JOIN o.customer c
        WHERE o.status = :status
        ORDER BY o.createdAt DESC
    """)
    Page<OrderSummary> findSummaries(@Param("status") OrderStatus status, Pageable page);
}
```

One query, joined with customer, flat DTO — no N+1 possible.

**Pattern 3 — dynamic projection for reuse:**

```java
public interface OrderRepository extends JpaRepository<Order, Long> {
    <T> List<T> findByStatus(OrderStatus status, Class<T> type);
}

List<OrderListItem> lightweight = orderRepo.findByStatus(PAID, OrderListItem.class);
List<OrderDetailDto> detailed   = orderRepo.findByStatus(PAID, OrderDetailDto.class);
List<Order>          entities   = orderRepo.findByStatus(PAID, Order.class);
```

**Pattern 4 — native query with class-based record:**

```java
public record CustomerRevenue(Long customerId, String customerName, BigDecimal revenue) {}

public interface ReportRepository extends Repository<Order, Long> {
    @Query(value = """
        SELECT c.id AS customerId, c.name AS customerName,
               SUM(o.total_amount) AS revenue
        FROM orders o JOIN customers c ON c.id = o.customer_id
        WHERE o.status = 'PAID' AND o.created_at >= :from
        GROUP BY c.id, c.name
        ORDER BY revenue DESC
    """, nativeQuery = true)
    List<CustomerRevenue> topCustomers(@Param("from") Instant from, Pageable page);
}
```

Column aliases match record component names — Spring maps by name.

**Correct bulk UPDATE with explicit audit fields:**

```java
public interface OrderRepository extends JpaRepository<Order, Long> {

    @Modifying(clearAutomatically = true, flushAutomatically = true)
    @Query("""
        UPDATE Order o
        SET o.status = :newStatus,
            o.lastModifiedAt = CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,
            o.lastModifiedBy = :actor
        WHERE o.status = :oldStatus AND o.createdAt < :cutoff
    """)
    int archiveByStatusAndAge(@Param("newStatus") OrderStatus newStatus,
                              @Param("oldStatus") OrderStatus oldStatus,
                              @Param("cutoff") Instant cutoff,
                              @Param("actor") String actor);
}
```

`@LastModifiedDate` does NOT fire — set the audit columns explicitly in JPQL.

**Bulk DELETE with cascade-aware pattern:**

```java
// WRONG: does NOT cascade to order_items even if Order has cascade=REMOVE
@Modifying
@Query("DELETE FROM Order o WHERE o.status = 'CANCELLED' AND o.createdAt < :cutoff")
int purgeCancelled(@Param("cutoff") Instant cutoff);
// Database throws FK violation

// RIGHT: delete children first, then parents
@Transactional
public int purgeCancelled(Instant cutoff) {
    int itemsDeleted = itemRepo.deleteItemsForCancelledOrders(cutoff);
    int ordersDeleted = orderRepo.deleteCancelledOrders(cutoff);
    return ordersDeleted;
}

@Modifying(clearAutomatically = true)
@Query("DELETE FROM OrderItem i WHERE i.order.status = 'CANCELLED' AND i.order.createdAt < :cutoff")
int deleteItemsForCancelledOrders(@Param("cutoff") Instant cutoff);

@Modifying(clearAutomatically = true)
@Query("DELETE FROM Order o WHERE o.status = 'CANCELLED' AND o.createdAt < :cutoff")
int deleteCancelledOrders(@Param("cutoff") Instant cutoff);
```

**Batch-updating thousands of rows with entity loading (audit-required):**

```java
@Transactional
public void archiveWithAudit(Instant cutoff) {
    int batchSize = 100;
    int page = 0;
    while (true) {
        List<Order> batch = orderRepo.findByStatusAndCreatedAtBefore(
            OPEN, cutoff, PageRequest.of(page, batchSize));
        if (batch.isEmpty()) break;

        batch.forEach(o -> o.setStatus(ARCHIVED));

        em.flush();
        em.clear();
        page++;
    }
}
```

With `spring.jpa.properties.hibernate.jdbc.batch_size: 50`, Hibernate groups UPDATEs into JDBC batches. Slower than pure `@Modifying` but all callbacks fire.
